findings.description:
Every commit is written as `- [ ] Commit: ...`. Commit markers must be plain `**Commit**:` lines, never checkboxes. They are also attached to ten or more individual tasks, including red points (see the previous finding), rather than working, tested points. A natural set of markers would be:
- after Task 1, the dependency bump;
- after Tasks 2 to 5, once the classification and translation changes have their tests;
- after Tasks 6 to 11, once the re-key work is green;
- at closeout.

Task 13's `chore: format and lint pass` commit may also be empty.

Task 10 bundles five test rewrites, four deletions, a docstring and comment cleanup, and a new test. It has no success check for the `allowed_warning` test beyond the final pytest run. Splitting it as described above would give it a clearer scope:
- shim test deletion goes to Task 9;
- rewrites onto `RateLimitEvent` stay in Task 10;
- the new informational-case test becomes a separate task or sub-item with its own pytest command.
